{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And the priest shall put it on the head of the one being purified, and the priest shall make atonement for him before YHWH.</b> Through the act of the blood of the guilt-offering and the oil, this atonement shall be effected. And likewise, 'to make atonement for him before YHWH' (Leviticus 14:29). And in Torat Kohanim (chapter 3, halakhah 12), regarding 'and the remaining oil that is on the priest's palm he shall put on the head of the one being purified and make atonement' — if he put it, he made atonement, and if he did not put it, he did not make atonement; these are the words of Rabbi Akiva. Rabbi Yohanan ben Nuri says: they are the remnant of a mitzvah; whether he put it or did not put it, he made atonement. If so, according to the opinion of Rabbi Yohanan ben Nuri, 'and he shall make atonement for him' refers to the guilt-offering itself.\n\nNow, the verse states regarding the guilt-offering 'and he shall make atonement,' and then it returns and says (Leviticus 14:19) regarding the sin-offering 'and the priest shall make atonement for the one being purified from his impurity,' and it says further regarding the burnt-offering and the meal-offering (Leviticus 14:20) 'and the priest shall make atonement for him and he shall be clean,' and we do not know what the subject of all these atonements is. Perhaps the guilt-offering makes atonement for the trespass he committed before the plague-strike afflicted him, and the sin-offering for the sin he sinned during the days of his affliction — perhaps in his distress he uttered a complaint against Elohim, and this is the sense of 'from his impurity' — and the burnt-offering and the meal-offering serve him as a ransom of the soul, that he may merit to become pure and return to his tent; therefore it says, 'and the priest shall make atonement for him and he shall be clean.'\n\nAnd in Torat Kohanim (ibid., halakhah 13), regarding 'and the priest shall perform the sin-offering and make atonement' — what does this teach? Since it is written, 'and the priest shall offer up the burnt-offering and the meal-offering upon the altar,' one might think they all hold him back (i.e., are all indispensable); therefore Scripture says, 'and the priest shall perform the sin-offering and make atonement' — this teaches that atonement depends on the sin-offering.\n\nAnd it is possible that 'and the priest shall make atonement for him and he shall be clean' is a general reference to everything performed on his behalf, for the birds too serve for atonement and purification, as it says regarding the plague-affliction of the house (Leviticus 14:53), 'and he shall make atonement for the house and it shall be clean.'"], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "The phrase 'perhaps in his distress he uttered a complaint against Elohim' reads תפלה (a prayer or utterance) literally; the context suggests a complaint or murmuring, but the word is translated literally here."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:54:00.611415625Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}